```
This job simply logs a message to the console when executed.

Next, we'll create a MonoBehaviour script that schedules the job and waits for it to complete:
```csharp
using Unity.Collections;
using Unity.Jobs;
using UnityEngine;

public class JobSystemTest : MonoBehaviour
{
    private JobHandle _jobHandle;

    private void Start()
    {
        // Schedule the job
        var job = new LogJob();
        _jobHandle = job.Schedule();
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        // Wait for the job to complete
        if (_jobHandle.IsCompleted)
        {
            // Dispose of any allocated memory
            _jobHandle.Complete();
        }
    }

    private void OnDestroy()
    {
        // Dispose of any allocated memory
        _jobHandle.Complete();
    }
}
```
In the `Start()` method, we create a new instance of the `LogJob` struct and schedule it to run using the `Schedule()` method. This schedules the job to be executed by the Unity job system.

In the `Update()` method, we check if the job has completed using the `IsCompleted` property. If it has, we call `Complete()` to dispose of any allocated memory.

Finally, in the `OnDestroy()` method, we call `Complete()` again to ensure that any allocated memory is disposed of when the script is destroyed.

That's it! This is a very basic implementation of a job system in Unity, but it should give you an idea of how to get started.

