The Lombok library provides the `@NonNull` annotation that can be used to mark a parameter, field, or method return value as not null. The implementation code for this annotation is generated by Lombok at compile-time, so there is no explicit implementation code that you need to write.

When you use the `@NonNull` annotation on a parameter, field, or method return value, Lombok generates a null-check for that element. For example, if you annotate a method parameter with `@NonNull`, Lombok generates code that checks if the parameter is null before executing the method body. If the parameter is null, Lombok throws a `NullPointerException` with a message that indicates which parameter was null.

Here is an example of how to use the `@NonNull` annotation on a method parameter:
```less
import lombok.NonNull;

public class Example {
    public void doSomething(@NonNull String param) {
        // Lombok generates null-check code for param here
        System.out.println(param);
    }
}
```
